00:08:17.000 --> 00:09:37.000
Between 1877 and 1950, 22 people were lynched in Jackson's Hines County, more than anywhere else in the state. In 1954, the federal governments forced end to segregation resulted in Jackson becoming more racially diverse. This prompted many white people to move away from the city, rendering some neighborhoods of Jackson almost exclusively black. One of the most infamous hate crimes that occurred in Mississippi was the 1955 murder of 14-year-old Emmett Till, a black teenager who allegedly woof-whistled at a white woman. In retaliation for this perceived slight, two white men kidnapped and killed Emmett before dumping his body into the Tallahatchie River. An all-white jury acquitted the men, even though they later admitted to the murder. Another race-fuelled incident occurred in 1964, when two 19-year-old black men, Charles Moore and Henry D., were murdered by members of the white supremacist hate group, the Ku Klux Klan, in the Mississippi town of Roxy.
在1877年至1950年间，杰克逊所在的海因斯县有22人被私刑处死，比该州其他任何地方都多。1954年，联邦政府强行结束种族隔离，导致杰克逊的种族变得更加多元化。这促使许多白人搬离该市，使得杰克逊的一些社区几乎全都是黑人。密西西比州发生的最臭名昭著的仇恨犯罪之一是1955年14岁埃米特·蒂尔的谋杀案，他是一名黑人少年，据称曾对一名白人女性吹口哨。为了报复这种被认为的轻蔑，两名白人男子绑架并杀害了埃米特，然后将他的尸体扔进了塔拉哈奇河。一个全白人的陪审团判处这些人无罪，尽管他们后来承认了谋杀。另一起由种族引发的事件发生在1964年，当时两名19岁的黑人男子查尔斯·摩尔和亨利·D，在密西西比州的罗克西镇被白人至上主义仇恨团体三K党的成员谋杀。

